                    REVERSE ENGINEERING COUNTERMEASURES PROCEDURES

The reverse engineering counter measures are several options that are built
into the encoder. There are two classes of revengcm. Revengcm options make it
more difficult to disassemble the output code and/or trace it.

There are two types of revengcm options. The first type can be invoked at any
time, without changing the run result of the program. The second type does
affect the way the program work, and requires compensation by the program to
return it to working status.

The revengcm package is designed to be removed completely for distribution
packages, from the encoder. This is done by swapping the module file
revengcm.pas for an enabled or disabled version. The enable or disable is done
with if/then statements, so it is important that the dead code elimination
option ('dce' or 'deadcodeeliminate') be enabled on production versions so that
the code is completely removed from the output.

Each option, and the consequences of its use, will be covered in this file.
This file, and all of th revengcm procedures and methods, should be kept
internal and never released.

#cxt, #charactertransliterate ==================================================

This option causes the constant strings in the constant area to be encoded with
a user specified pattern. This makes it more difficult to find strings in the
code, because the will no longer be in ASCII code.

With this option enabled, the encoder will look for a file by the name:

chrtrn.xlt

In the current directory. An error will occur, and compilation will stop, if it
is not found.

chrtrn.xlt is a text file, containing N lines of the following format:

A*
F&
NY
BT
...

Each line contains two characters. The first is the original ASCII character
that is to be re-encoded. The second is what it should encode to. Any other
characters on the line are ignored. There is no arrangements for quoting or
comments in the file.

Any number of transliteration entries may appear, in any order. If a character
is not indicated for encoding, it is output unchanged. Any character in the
range 0..255 may be specified, but of course it is difficult to specify
control characters using the current setup.

Any transliteration can be specified. However, there are some important
attributes that might limit the choices that are acceptable.

1. Space not encoded. If spaces are not encoded, the padding procedures in
strlib will still work on the encoded strings.

2. No control character translation. This is easier to work with, and control
characters are less obvious in the code in any case.

3. Sequential codes. If the series 'a'..'z', 'A'..'Z' and '0'..9' are kept
sequential in the code, the strlib procedures to sort for order will still
work.

At the present, we use a code that does not encode spaces or control characters,
but is not sequential. This allows most of the strlib procedures and hashing
to work, but not the compare procedures.

The character transliteration option DOES affect the way the program runs, and
the program must compensate for it. First of all, only multiple character string
constants are encoded. For example:

writeln('hello');

Would output garbage, since the string 'hello' would be encoded. However:

writeln('h', 'e', 'l', 'l', 'o');

Would work, because it is a series of single character constants.

When working with strings, the normal or encoded status must be compensated for.
If a encoded string is to be output, it must be decoded first. If two strings
are to be compared (for equality), they must either both be encoded, or neither
encoded. This applies to hashing, as well.

A hidden effect exists in the use of header files:

program test(input, output, myfile);

...

would NOT work, because the filenames "_input", "_output", and "_myfile" would
be generated internally to handle the header files. Instead, you must open these
files manually, using the assign, reset and/or rewrite procedures.

To make use of transliterated files easier, a module exists:

xltlib

Which contains the following procedures and functions:

function nrmchr(c): char;

   Translate from encoded character to normal character.

procedure nrmstr(var s: string);

   Translate string from encoded characters to normal characters.

function xltchr(c): char;

   Translate from normal charcter to encoded character.

procedure xltstr(var s: string);

   Translate string from normal characters to encoded characters.

There are two versions of this module,

xltlib_xlt
xltlib_nrm

That are copied to the xltlib file. The first, xltlib_xlt, peforms the encode
and decode mentioned above. The file xltlib_nrm is a no-op, and simply returns
what it is sent. What this allows is the swapping of a module file so that both
non-encoded and encoded files can be used.

Encoded and non-encoded objects can be freely intermixed. It is up to the
programmer, however, to resolve what constants come from an encoded file, and
what constants come from a normal file. The best policy is to ensure that 
encoded strings never leave the encoded file.

#discm, #disassemblycountermeasures ===========================================

This option causes disassembly countermeasures to be placed in the code. 
Disassembly countermeasures are features that only make sense when executed,
and a disassembler cannot follow. For example, after each unconditional
control transfer, a disassembler must assume that the next instruction is valid
code. Instead, a very short leader to a very long instruction is placed there,
causing a disassembler to throw away several instructions before falling back
into sync.

These "opportunity" slots are filled, and also, at random, a deliberate jump
over is inserted in-line to code. The result is a general disabling of proper
code disassembly.

The in-line code generated is completely without effect to the run of the 
program, other than negatively affecting its speed. The frequency of such code
trips is ballanced between impacting code speed, and being frequent enough to
cause significant disassembly problems.

===============================================================================

FUTURE RESEARCH

* Use of "cmp r32,imm32" type of instructions, where a flow joins at the imm32.
The imm32 can contain no-ops, or even valid instructions, if it can be 
determined that a simple sequence fits there.

* Checksums or crcs of the program code section for a module. This may not need
to be built into the encoder. For example, a .exe examination program can
determine the entire chksum/crc for the program section, and implace that in
the program for the check.
